1
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:12,987
December 4th,

2
00:00:12,987 --> 00:00:13,880
1872

3
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:15,240
In the middle of the Atlantic,

4
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:17,760
sailors spot a ship drifting where no ship

5
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:18,551
should be

6
00:00:18,701 --> 00:00:19,731
Her sails are torn

7
00:00:19,731 --> 00:00:20,971
No one is at the wheel

8
00:00:20,971 --> 00:00:22,798
No one answers their calls

9
00:00:22,948 --> 00:00:24,618
They row across and climb aboard

10
00:00:24,618 --> 00:00:25,338
There is food

11
00:00:25,338 --> 00:00:26,298
There is fresh water

12
00:00:26,298 --> 00:00:28,008
The cargo is still there

13
00:00:28,158 --> 00:00:29,908
But all ten people on board are gone,

14
00:00:29,908 --> 00:00:31,760
and they were never seen again

15
00:00:31,910 --> 00:00:34,020
This is the true story of the Mary Celeste,

16
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:36,620
the most famous ghost ship in history

17
00:00:39,817 --> 00:00:43,247
She was born in 1861 in a tiny shipyard

18
00:00:43,247 --> 00:00:44,287
in Nova Scotia,

19
00:00:44,287 --> 00:00:44,927
Canada,

20
00:00:44,927 --> 00:00:46,007
under a different name:

21
00:00:46,007 --> 00:00:47,127
Amazon

22
00:00:47,515 --> 00:00:48,825
Her luck was bad from the start

23
00:00:48,825 --> 00:00:51,105
Her first captain fell ill and died just days

24
00:00:51,105 --> 00:00:52,865
after taking command

25
00:00:53,065 --> 00:00:54,095
In the years that followed,

26
00:00:54,095 --> 00:00:56,215
she hit fishing gear off the coast of Maine

27
00:00:56,215 --> 00:00:58,175
and collided with another ship in the English

28
00:00:58,175 --> 00:00:58,615
Channel,

29
00:00:58,615 --> 00:00:59,916
sinking it

30
00:01:00,066 --> 00:01:02,056
In 1867,

31
00:01:02,056 --> 00:01:04,016
a storm drove her onto the rocks,

32
00:01:04,016 --> 00:01:06,117
and she was left as a wreck

33
00:01:06,267 --> 00:01:08,057
An American buyer bought what was left,

34
00:01:08,057 --> 00:01:09,977
rebuilt her and gave her a new name:

35
00:01:09,977 --> 00:01:11,737
the Mary Celeste

36
00:01:12,037 --> 00:01:14,547
Sailors say renaming a ship brings bad luck

37
00:01:14,547 --> 00:01:15,627
A few years later,

38
00:01:15,627 --> 00:01:18,227
she set out on her most famous voyage

39
00:01:22,083 --> 00:01:23,893
Her new captain was Benjamin Briggs,

40
00:01:23,893 --> 00:01:24,913
thirty-seven years old,

41
00:01:24,913 --> 00:01:27,353
an experienced sailor from a family of sea

42
00:01:27,353 --> 00:01:28,473
captains

43
00:01:28,748 --> 00:01:29,698
He wasn't going alone

44
00:01:29,698 --> 00:01:31,698
His wife Sarah and their two-year-old daughter

45
00:01:31,698 --> 00:01:32,738
Sophia came along

46
00:01:32,738 --> 00:01:34,658
Their seven-year-old son stayed home for

47
00:01:34,658 --> 00:01:35,727
school

48
00:01:35,877 --> 00:01:37,027
Seven crewmen joined them

49
00:01:37,027 --> 00:01:38,711
Ten people in all

50
00:01:38,861 --> 00:01:41,971
In the hold were 1,701 barrels of industrial

51
00:01:41,971 --> 00:01:42,651
alcohol,

52
00:01:42,651 --> 00:01:45,016
bound for Genoa in Italy

53
00:01:45,166 --> 00:01:46,316
According to one story,

54
00:01:46,316 --> 00:01:47,596
the night before he sailed,

55
00:01:47,596 --> 00:01:49,356
Briggs had dinner with an old friend,

56
00:01:49,356 --> 00:01:51,116
Captain David Morehouse

57
00:01:51,668 --> 00:01:52,538
Morehouse's ship,

58
00:01:52,538 --> 00:01:54,098
the Dei Gratia,

59
00:01:54,098 --> 00:01:56,258
would follow almost the same route about a week

60
00:01:56,258 --> 00:01:56,538
later

61
00:01:56,538 --> 00:01:58,498
Neither man knew how their paths would cross

62
00:01:58,498 --> 00:01:59,618
again

63
00:02:02,983 --> 00:02:04,946
On November 7th,

64
00:02:04,946 --> 00:02:05,893
1872,

65
00:02:05,893 --> 00:02:08,453
the Mary Celeste sailed out of New York into

66
00:02:08,453 --> 00:02:09,985
rough autumn weather

67
00:02:10,135 --> 00:02:11,125
For more than two weeks,

68
00:02:11,125 --> 00:02:13,725
she sailed east across the open Atlantic

69
00:02:14,118 --> 00:02:15,348
On November 25th,

70
00:02:15,348 --> 00:02:16,468
at eight in the morning,

71
00:02:16,468 --> 00:02:19,068
someone made a short entry in the ship's log

72
00:02:19,506 --> 00:02:20,576
They were passing Santa Maria,

73
00:02:20,576 --> 00:02:21,916
an island in the Azores,

74
00:02:21,916 --> 00:02:23,559
far out in the ocean

75
00:02:23,709 --> 00:02:24,819
Everything seemed normal

76
00:02:24,819 --> 00:02:27,059
But it was the last entry anyone aboard would

77
00:02:27,059 --> 00:02:28,499
ever write

78
00:02:31,983 --> 00:02:32,893
Nine days later,

79
00:02:32,893 --> 00:02:35,533
the Dei Gratia was crossing the same stretch of

80
00:02:35,533 --> 00:02:38,133
ocean between the Azores and Portugal

81
00:02:38,473 --> 00:02:40,263
One of the crew spotted a ship moving

82
00:02:40,263 --> 00:02:40,904
strangely,

83
00:02:40,904 --> 00:02:43,220
drifting off course with only some of her sails

84
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:44,187
up

85
00:02:44,337 --> 00:02:46,287
Captain Morehouse watched her for a long time

86
00:02:46,287 --> 00:02:47,407
As they came closer,

87
00:02:47,407 --> 00:02:49,127
they realised which ship it was:

88
00:02:49,127 --> 00:02:50,887
the Mary Celeste

89
00:02:51,226 --> 00:02:52,256
There was no one on deck,

90
00:02:52,256 --> 00:02:54,432
and no answer to their signals

91
00:02:54,582 --> 00:02:55,732
He sent his first mate,

92
00:02:55,732 --> 00:02:56,732
Oliver Deveau,

93
00:02:56,732 --> 00:02:57,852
and his second mate,

94
00:02:57,852 --> 00:02:58,532
John Wright,

95
00:02:58,532 --> 00:03:00,932
across in a small boat

96
00:03:04,150 --> 00:03:06,260
Deveau climbed aboard and called out

97
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:07,380
Silence

98
00:03:07,803 --> 00:03:09,553
There was about a metre of water sloshing in

99
00:03:09,553 --> 00:03:09,993
the hold

100
00:03:09,993 --> 00:03:10,393
Not good,

101
00:03:10,393 --> 00:03:12,704
but nowhere near enough to sink her

102
00:03:12,854 --> 00:03:14,484
One of the pumps had been taken apart,

103
00:03:14,484 --> 00:03:17,084
and a makeshift measuring rod lay on the deck

104
00:03:17,603 --> 00:03:18,193
Below deck,

105
00:03:18,193 --> 00:03:19,153
everything was damp

106
00:03:19,153 --> 00:03:19,793
But clothes,

107
00:03:19,793 --> 00:03:22,393
books and personal things were still in place

108
00:03:22,933 --> 00:03:25,043
There was enough food and fresh water for six

109
00:03:25,043 --> 00:03:25,443
months

110
00:03:25,443 --> 00:03:27,817
And the cargo of alcohol was still in the

111
00:03:27,817 --> 00:03:28,762
hold

112
00:03:28,912 --> 00:03:30,622
But the ship's only lifeboat was gone

113
00:03:30,622 --> 00:03:32,782
So were the captain's navigation instruments

114
00:03:32,782 --> 00:03:35,382
and most of the ship's papers

115
00:03:38,250 --> 00:03:39,360
No sign of a fight

116
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:40,600
No sign of a fire

117
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:42,360
Nothing valuable taken

118
00:03:42,709 --> 00:03:44,819
Someone had launched the lifeboat and taken the

119
00:03:44,819 --> 00:03:47,419
tools needed to find their way at sea

120
00:03:47,678 --> 00:03:49,108
That means they left on purpose,

121
00:03:49,108 --> 00:03:51,163
and probably in a hurry

122
00:03:51,313 --> 00:03:53,303
But why would an experienced captain put his

123
00:03:53,303 --> 00:03:55,823
wife and baby daughter into a small open boat

124
00:03:55,823 --> 00:03:57,804
in the middle of the Atlantic?

125
00:03:57,954 --> 00:04:00,554
And why abandon a ship that could still sail?

126
00:04:00,849 --> 00:04:03,439
Deveau and two men sailed the empty ship all

127
00:04:03,439 --> 00:04:04,799
the way to Gibraltar

128
00:04:04,799 --> 00:04:05,039
There,

129
00:04:05,039 --> 00:04:07,439
the questions turned into suspicion

130
00:04:11,050 --> 00:04:11,920
In Gibraltar,

131
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:14,360
the Dei Gratia's crew asked for a salvage

132
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:16,636
reward for bringing the ship in

133
00:04:16,786 --> 00:04:17,776
But the attorney general,

134
00:04:17,776 --> 00:04:19,056
Frederick Solly-Flood,

135
00:04:19,056 --> 00:04:20,688
smelled a crime

136
00:04:20,838 --> 00:04:21,428
Was it mutiny?

137
00:04:21,428 --> 00:04:21,868
Murder?

138
00:04:21,868 --> 00:04:23,588
Had the two captains planned the whole thing

139
00:04:23,588 --> 00:04:24,708
together?

140
00:04:24,879 --> 00:04:27,149
Investigators examined marks on the ship and

141
00:04:27,149 --> 00:04:29,309
stains on a sword found under the captain's

142
00:04:29,309 --> 00:04:30,221
bed

143
00:04:30,371 --> 00:04:32,921
Later tests showed the stains were not blood,

144
00:04:32,921 --> 00:04:35,521
and no evidence of a crime was ever found

145
00:04:36,339 --> 00:04:37,489
The salvors were paid,

146
00:04:37,489 --> 00:04:39,929
but only about one fifth of the ship's value

147
00:04:39,929 --> 00:04:42,529
The doubts never fully went away

148
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:46,780
Eleven years later,

149
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:49,500
a young doctor wrote a short story inspired by

150
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:50,744
the case

151
00:04:50,894 --> 00:04:52,444
His name was Arthur Conan Doyle,

152
00:04:52,444 --> 00:04:55,044
the man who would later create Sherlock Holmes

153
00:04:55,608 --> 00:04:56,278
In his story,

154
00:04:56,278 --> 00:04:58,198
the ship was called the Marie Celeste,

155
00:04:58,198 --> 00:04:59,878
and the table was set with food,

156
00:04:59,878 --> 00:05:01,718
as if the crew had vanished in the middle

157
00:05:01,718 --> 00:05:02,842
of a meal

158
00:05:02,992 --> 00:05:04,542
Many readers believed it was true,

159
00:05:04,542 --> 00:05:06,512
and the details stuck

160
00:05:06,662 --> 00:05:07,252
Even today,

161
00:05:07,252 --> 00:05:09,172
you'll hear about half-eaten breakfasts and

162
00:05:09,172 --> 00:05:11,574
cups of tea still warm on the table

163
00:05:11,724 --> 00:05:13,434
None of that was in the real reports

164
00:05:13,434 --> 00:05:16,034
The real mystery is strange enough on its own

165
00:05:19,683 --> 00:05:20,433
Over the years,

166
00:05:20,433 --> 00:05:22,833
people have blamed almost everything

167
00:05:23,375 --> 00:05:23,845
Pirates?

168
00:05:23,845 --> 00:05:25,605
But nothing valuable was taken,

169
00:05:25,605 --> 00:05:27,916
and there was no sign of a struggle

170
00:05:28,066 --> 00:05:28,816
A drunken crew?

171
00:05:28,816 --> 00:05:30,176
The alcohol was industrial

172
00:05:30,176 --> 00:05:32,584
Drinking it would have made them very sick

173
00:05:32,734 --> 00:05:33,804
A giant squid,

174
00:05:33,804 --> 00:05:34,444
a waterspout,

175
00:05:34,444 --> 00:05:36,004
an undersea earthquake?

176
00:05:36,004 --> 00:05:38,448
There's no real evidence for any of them

177
00:05:38,598 --> 00:05:39,508
To find the answer,

178
00:05:39,508 --> 00:05:41,828
investigators went back to the boring details

179
00:05:41,828 --> 00:05:43,908
And the most important clue was hiding in the

180
00:05:43,908 --> 00:05:45,028
cargo

181
00:05:48,617 --> 00:05:51,007
When the cargo was finally unloaded in Genoa,

182
00:05:51,007 --> 00:05:53,607
nine of the 1,701 barrels were empty

183
00:05:54,829 --> 00:05:56,299
Most barrels were made of white oak

184
00:05:56,299 --> 00:05:58,299
The nine empty ones were made of red oak,

185
00:05:58,299 --> 00:06:00,577
a more porous wood that can leak

186
00:06:00,727 --> 00:06:02,837
Alcohol fumes may have built up in the closed

187
00:06:02,837 --> 00:06:03,157
hold

188
00:06:03,157 --> 00:06:04,677
The fore hatch was found open,

189
00:06:04,677 --> 00:06:07,277
as if someone had tried to air it out

190
00:06:07,438 --> 00:06:08,908
If Briggs feared an explosion,

191
00:06:08,908 --> 00:06:11,068
getting his family off the ship would have made

192
00:06:11,068 --> 00:06:12,177
sense

193
00:06:12,327 --> 00:06:13,397
In 2006,

194
00:06:13,397 --> 00:06:16,117
chemist Andrea Sella tested the idea with a

195
00:06:16,117 --> 00:06:18,331
model hold in a London lab

196
00:06:18,481 --> 00:06:20,871
A small vapour explosion made a frightening

197
00:06:20,871 --> 00:06:21,271
blast,

198
00:06:21,271 --> 00:06:23,871
but left almost no burn marks

199
00:06:27,383 --> 00:06:28,613
Another investigation,

200
00:06:28,613 --> 00:06:30,333
filmed in 2007,

201
00:06:30,333 --> 00:06:31,133
looked at the weather,

202
00:06:31,133 --> 00:06:33,733
the ship's position and its equipment

203
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:34,630
Its conclusion:

204
00:06:34,630 --> 00:06:37,550
Briggs may have been about 120 miles west of

205
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:38,550
where he thought he was,

206
00:06:38,550 --> 00:06:41,096
perhaps because of a faulty chronometer

207
00:06:41,246 --> 00:06:42,516
The ship had just been rebuilt,

208
00:06:42,516 --> 00:06:44,116
and debris could have clogged the pumps

209
00:06:44,116 --> 00:06:45,836
That would explain the pump that was taken

210
00:06:45,836 --> 00:06:46,914
apart

211
00:06:47,064 --> 00:06:48,974
If Briggs believed his ship was sinking,

212
00:06:48,974 --> 00:06:51,254
and Santa Maria was the closest land,

213
00:06:51,254 --> 00:06:53,014
abandoning ship might have looked like the

214
00:06:53,014 --> 00:06:54,454
safest choice

215
00:06:54,657 --> 00:06:56,407
Maybe they tied the lifeboat to the ship with

216
00:06:56,407 --> 00:06:57,287
a rope and waited

217
00:06:57,287 --> 00:06:59,050
Then the wind picked up

218
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:00,230
We'll never know for sure

219
00:07:00,230 --> 00:07:02,830
But one more strange chapter was still to come

220
00:07:06,750 --> 00:07:07,660
After the mystery,

221
00:07:07,660 --> 00:07:10,380
the Mary Celeste kept sailing for twelve more

222
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:10,700
years

223
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:12,660
She changed owners again and again,

224
00:07:12,660 --> 00:07:15,260
and her bad reputation followed her

225
00:07:15,528 --> 00:07:17,918
In January 1885,

226
00:07:17,918 --> 00:07:20,198
her last captain deliberately ran her onto a

227
00:07:20,198 --> 00:07:21,764
reef off Haiti

228
00:07:21,914 --> 00:07:23,464
The plan was insurance fraud:

229
00:07:23,464 --> 00:07:25,584
the cargo had been insured for far more than

230
00:07:25,584 --> 00:07:26,768
it was worth

231
00:07:26,918 --> 00:07:27,948
The plot was exposed,

232
00:07:27,948 --> 00:07:29,828
and the famous ship ended her life as a

233
00:07:29,828 --> 00:07:31,204
crime scene

234
00:07:31,354 --> 00:07:32,264
In 2001,

235
00:07:32,264 --> 00:07:34,184
explorers announced they had found her wreck

236
00:07:34,184 --> 00:07:36,264
But tests on the wood showed the trees were

237
00:07:36,264 --> 00:07:37,864
still growing years after she sank

238
00:07:37,864 --> 00:07:39,250
It wasn't her

239
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:41,270
The ten people from the lifeboat were never

240
00:07:41,270 --> 00:07:42,390
found

241
00:07:43,083 --> 00:07:43,993
A seaworthy ship

242
00:07:43,993 --> 00:07:45,193
Food for six months

243
00:07:45,193 --> 00:07:47,193
And ten people who chose to leave it all

244
00:07:47,193 --> 00:07:48,054
behind

245
00:07:48,204 --> 00:07:48,914
Leaking fumes,

246
00:07:48,914 --> 00:07:49,914
a faulty clock,

247
00:07:49,914 --> 00:07:52,061
or something we haven't thought of yet?

248
00:07:52,211 --> 00:07:53,701
What do you think happened aboard the Mary

249
00:07:53,701 --> 00:07:54,161
Celeste?

250
00:07:54,161 --> 00:07:55,789
Tell me in the comments

251
00:07:55,939 --> 00:07:58,539
Subscribe so you don't miss the next episode
